--TEST--
A `Core\Db\Write`'s three readings differ in what may be missing — `affected` is a `uint` and the other two are `?uint` — so a count that does not exist is not silently a zero, `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound asserted on both sides. `affected` always answers a number, so it
// needs no unwrapping and `$w->affected() == 0` is a real question. `changed`
// is that same count *as the server reported it*, and its `null` is the one
// thing `affected` cannot say: this statement's kind carries no row count at
// all, which a `create table` is. `lastId` is `null` wherever the statement
// handed no key back, which is every statement with no `RETURNING` clause.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $made = $db->execute("create table accounts (id bigint, owner text)", []);

// The accepted half: no unwrapping, because there is nothing absent to unwrap.
uint $rows = $made->affected();

// The refused half, on the same object and three lines apart, which is the
// whole claim: PHP's `rowCount` answers `0` both for "affected nothing" and
// for "has no count to give", and those are different facts.
uint $reported = $made->changed();

// The other `?uint`, spelled the way a caller has to spell it.
?uint $lastId = $made->lastId();
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `uint`, found `uint|null`
   --> case.nvs:17:18
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
